 --push` pushes tags only for packages changed by that release. It pushes one tag at a time because GitHub doesn't trigger workflows for a push of more than three tags. wp-release doesn't create GitHub Releases: with `--json` or `--github-output`, each entry of `tags` has the `tag`, a `title` like `plugins/alpha@1.2.0` or, for a package at the repository root, the release title like `1.2.0` (see releaseTitle, and `--release-title` for overriding it in one command), the release `notes` and a `prerelease` flag, for a workflow step like `softprops/action-gh-release` to create the release (see the example below). For initial adoption, preview all missing tags with `changeset-tag --dry-run` (without `--since`), then push if needed. To release each package with a separate workflow instead, `changeset-tag --since <merge-commit>^1 --untagged --github-output` lists the releases that don't have a tag yet, without creating any. Tag pushes need Git write credentials."],[572,"After merging the release PR, `changeset-tag --since <merge-commit>^1 --push` pushes tags only for packages changed by that release. It pushes one tag at a time because GitHub doesn't trigger workflows for a push of more than three tags. wp-release doesn't create GitHub Releases: with `--json` or `--github-output`, each entry of `tags` has the `tag`, a `title` like `plugins/alpha@1.2.0` or, for a package at the repository root, the release title like `1.2.0` (see releaseTitle, and `--release-title` for overriding it in one command), the release `notes` and a `prerelease` flag, for a workflow step like `softprops/action-gh-release` to create the release (see the example below). For initial adoption, preview all missing tags with `changeset-tag --dry-run` (without `--since`), then push if needed. To release each package with a separate workflow instead, `changeset-tag --since <merge-commit>^1 --untagged --github-output` lists the releases that don't have a tag yet, without creating any. Tag pushes need Git wr