r one command."],[2477,"The configuration lives in `changesets.config.php` at the project root, the analog of `wp-release.config.mjs`. The global `--config PATH` selects another file for one command."],[384,"The default output is `<Domain Path>/<text-domain>.pot`, or `languages/<text-domain>.pot`. A leading `/` in `Domain Path` is package-relative, and configured destinations cannot escape the package. `--destination` changes the package-relative directory. An explicit `--output <file.pot>` resolves against the command's working directory and requires one package. All multi-package destinations are validated before writing; each completed package replaces its file atomically, independently of other packages. `--check` compares bytes without writing and fails if the POT is absent or stale. Source references are preserved by default as `file:line`. `--add-location file` keeps only the file name, so that editing a source file doesn't change the POT unless its strings change, and `--no-location` (the same as `--add-location never`) omits them; `--add-location full` is the default. In configuration, set `location` to `'full'`, `'file'` or `'never'`. The older `--no-references` flag and `references: false` option still work as `--no-location`, and an explicit `location` wins over `references` in the same layer. Asking for locations and for none in the same command is an error. `POT-Creation-Date` and `PO-Revision-Date` default to empty values; no current date or year is inserted. Name/version headers come from metadata, and `--header NAME=VALUE` overrides any default, including with an empty value, and `--header NAME` removes the header. The POT lists the plugin or theme header strings first, then the strings of PHP, JavaScript, `block.json` and other metadata files in source order, so related strings stay together for translators. Each entry has its extracted comments, references and flags before the strings, in the usual gettext layout. The default headers come in a fix